NAME
    Statistics::CaseResampling - Efficient resampling and calculation of
    medians with confidence intervals

SYNOPSIS
      use Statistics::CaseResampling ':all';
  
      my $sample = [1,3,5,7,1,2,9,4,6,8]; # ... usually MUCH more data ...
      my $confidence = 0.95; # 95% confidence limits (~1.96*sigma)
      #my $confidence = 1 - nsigma_to_alpha(1); # ~1*sigma (~68.27% confidence limits)
  
      # calculate the median of the sample with distribution-free exact
      # binomial confidence limits:
      my ($lower_cl, $median, $upper_cl)
        = median_exact_confidence_limits($sample, $confidence);
  
      # or using percentile bootstrapping (defaults to 1000 resamples; use
      # 5000-10000+ for reliable tail limits):
      my ($lower_cl_boot, $median_boot, $upper_cl_boot)
        = median_percentile_confidence_limits($sample, $confidence, 5000);
  
      # or all-in-C BCa bootstrap confidence limits for the mean:
      my ($lower_bca, $mean_bca, $upper_bca)
        = mean_bca_confidence_limits($sample, $confidence, 5000);
  
      # There are many auxiliary functions:
  
      my $resampled = resample($sample);
      # $resampled is now a random set of measurements from $sample,
      # including potential duplicates
  
      my $medians = resample_medians($sample, $n_resamples);
      # $medians is now an array reference containing the medians
      # of $n_resamples resample runs
      # This is vastly more efficient than doing the same thing with
      # repeated resample() calls!
      # Analogously:
      my $means = resample_means($sample, $n_resamples);
  
      # you can get two-sided percentile (or basic, or BCa) confidence limits
      # from a set of separately resampled statistics, too:
      my ($lower_m, $mean, $upper_m)
        = percentile_confidence_limits_from_samples(mean($sample), $means, $confidence);
  
      # utility functions:
      print median([1..5]), "\n"; # prints 3
      print mean([1..5]), "\n"; # prints 3, too, surprise!
      print quantile([1..4], 0.5), "\n"; # prints 2.5
      print sample_standard_deviation($sample), "\n";
      print select_kth([1..5], 1), "\n"; # inefficient way to calculate the minimum

DESCRIPTION
    The purpose of this (XS) module is to calculate the median (or in
    principle also other statistics) with confidence intervals on a sample.
    To do that, it uses a technique called bootstrapping. In a nutshell, it
    resamples the sample a lot of times and for each resample, it calculates
    the statistic. From the distribution of resampled statistics, it then
    calculates the confidence limits.

    In order to implement the confidence limit calculation, various other
    functions had to be implemented efficiently (both algorithmically
    efficient and done in C). These functions may be useful in their own
    right and are thus exposed to Perl. Most notably, this exposes a median
    (and general selection) algorithm that works in linear time as opposed
    to the trivial implementation that requires "O(n*log(n))".

  Statistical caveats
    Case resampling (nonparametric bootstrap) assumes that the observations
    in $sample are independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.). If your
    data exhibits autocorrelation, time trends, or clustering, standard case
    resampling will underestimate uncertainty.

    Additionally, bootstrap confidence intervals for the median have limited
    coverage accuracy on very small samples (e.g. "N < 15") or when the data
    contains many tied values; prefer
    "median_exact_confidence_limits(SAMPLE, CONFIDENCE)" when a
    distribution-free confidence interval for the median is required.

  Input validation and error handling
    All functions reject "undef" and non-numeric values, both inside sample
    arrays and in scalar parameters (probabilities, confidence levels, run
    counts, and "erf"/"alpha"/"nsigma" arguments). Numeric strings, "NaN",
    and "Inf" are accepted; "NaN" propagates deterministically through
    summary statistics and order statistics regardless of element ordering.

    All summary statistic and confidence-limit functions croak on empty
    samples ("[]"). The raw resampling helpers (resample(),
    resample_medians(), resample_means(), and resample_quantiles()) return
    "[]" on an empty sample and require run counts in 1..100000000.

  Upgrading from 0.x to 1.00
    Version 1.00 fixes several long-standing statistical correctness bugs
    that change outputs for some legacy calls:

    *   simple_confidence_limits_from_samples() and
        median_simple_confidence_limits() now compute true two-sided
        "alpha/2" and "1 - alpha/2" tails with Type 7 quantile interpolation
        instead of one-sided "alpha" tails and truncated 1-based indices.

    *   median(), median_absolute_deviation(), resample_medians(),
        first_quartile(), and third_quartile() now use Hyndman-Fan Type 7
        interpolation (averaging the two middle elements for even "N").

    *   approx_erf_inv() now accepts its full domain "(-1, 1)",
        alpha_to_nsigma() requires "alpha" in "(0, 1]", and
        nsigma_to_alpha() requires "nsigma >= 0".

    *   Empty samples and "undef"/non-numeric inputs croak instead of
        silently returning or coercing to 0.

  Random numbers
    The resampling involves drawing many random numbers. Therefore, the
    module comes with an embedded Mersenne twister (taken from
    "Math::Random::MT").

    If you want to change the seed of the RNG, do this:

      $Statistics::CaseResampling::Rnd
        = Statistics::CaseResampling::RdGen::setup($seed);

    or

      $Statistics::CaseResampling::Rnd
        = Statistics::CaseResampling::RdGen::setup_array(@seed);

    The default module RNG is automatically seeded from native OS entropy
    ("BCryptGenRandom"/"RtlGenRandom" on Windows, "/dev/urandom" on Unix, or
    process/time/PRNG fallback state when unavailable) and is reseeded
    across "ithreads" cloning and fork(). Explicitly seeded RNG objects
    retain their exact deterministic sequence.

    Do not use the embedded random number generator for other purposes. Use
    "Math::Random::MT" instead! At this point, you cannot change the type of
    RNG.

  EXPORT
    None by default.

    Can export any of the functions that are documented below using standard
    "Exporter" semantics, including the customary ":all" group.

FUNCTIONS
    This list of functions is loosely sorted from *basic* to *comprehensive*
    because the more complicated functions are usually (under the hood, in
    C) implemented using the basic building blocks. Unfortunately, that
    means you may want to read the documentation backwards. :)

    All of these functions are written in C for speed.

  resample(ARRAYREF)
    Returns a reference to an array containing N random elements from the
    input array, where N is the length of the original array.

    This is different from shuffling in that it's random drawing without
    removing the drawn elements from the sample.

  resample_medians(ARRAYREF, NMEDIANS)
    Returns a reference to an array containing the medians of "NMEDIANS"
    resamples of the original input sample.

  resample_means(ARRAYREF, NMEANS)
    Returns a reference to an array containing the means of "NMEANS"
    resamples of the original input sample.

  resample_quantiles(ARRAYREF, P, NQUANTILES)
    Returns a reference to an array containing the Type 7 sample quantile at
    probability *P* in "[0, 1]" of "NQUANTILES" resamples of the original
    input sample.

  percentile_confidence_limits_from_samples(STATISTIC, STATISTIC_SAMPLES, CONFIDENCE)
    Calculates two-sided percentile bootstrap confidence limits at level
    *CONFIDENCE* (in "(0, 1)") using linear quantile interpolation at
    "alpha/2" and "1 - alpha/2", where "alpha = 1 - CONFIDENCE". Returns
    "($lower_cl, $statistic, $upper_cl)".

    For example, to get a 95% confidence interval for the mean of your
    sample:

      my $sample = [<numbers>];
      my $means = resample_means($sample, 5000);
      my ($lower_cl, $mean, $upper_cl)
        = percentile_confidence_limits_from_samples(mean($sample), $means, 0.95);

    For reliable tail limits at 95% or higher confidence, use at least
    5000-10000 bootstrap samples.

  basic_confidence_limits_from_samples(STATISTIC, STATISTIC_SAMPLES, CONFIDENCE)
    Calculates two-sided basic (pivotal) bootstrap confidence limits at
    level *CONFIDENCE* (in "(0, 1)") as "2 * STATISTIC -
    quantile(STATISTIC_SAMPLES, 1 - alpha/2)" and "2 * STATISTIC -
    quantile(STATISTIC_SAMPLES, alpha/2)", where "alpha = 1 - CONFIDENCE".
    Returns "($lower_cl, $statistic, $upper_cl)".

    Note that for heavily skewed distributions or bounded statistics (e.g.
    strictly positive measurements), the basic bootstrap reflection can
    produce limits outside the parameter support; prefer
    "percentile_confidence_limits_from_samples(STATISTIC, STATISTIC_SAMPLES,
    CONFIDENCE)" or "bca_confidence_limits_from_samples(STATISTIC,
    STATISTIC_SAMPLES, JACKKNIFE_SAMPLES, CONFIDENCE)" in those cases.

  bca_confidence_limits_from_samples(STATISTIC, STATISTIC_SAMPLES, JACKKNIFE_SAMPLES, CONFIDENCE)
    Calculates two-sided bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) bootstrap
    confidence limits at level *CONFIDENCE* (in "(0, 1)").
    *JACKKNIFE_SAMPLES* must be an array reference of leave-one-out
    jackknife estimates of the statistic on the original sample. Returns
    "($lower_cl, $statistic, $upper_cl)".

    BCa intervals correct for both median bias and skewness (via the
    acceleration constant) and are transformation-respecting.

  mean_percentile_confidence_limits(SAMPLE, CONFIDENCE, [NSAMPLES])
    All-in-C two-sided percentile bootstrap confidence limits for the
    arithmetic mean of *SAMPLE* at level *CONFIDENCE* in "(0, 1)".
    *NSAMPLES* defaults to 1000 (use 5000 or more for stable 95%+ limits).
    Returns "($lower_cl, $mean, $upper_cl)".

  mean_bca_confidence_limits(SAMPLE, CONFIDENCE, [NSAMPLES])
    All-in-C two-sided bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) bootstrap
    confidence limits for the arithmetic mean of *SAMPLE*, computing the
    leave-one-out jackknife means in O(n) time without Perl overhead.
    *NSAMPLES* defaults to 1000. Returns "($lower_cl, $mean, $upper_cl)".

  mean_studentized_confidence_limits(SAMPLE, CONFIDENCE, [NSAMPLES])
    All-in-C two-sided studentized (bootstrap-t) confidence limits for the
    arithmetic mean of *SAMPLE* (requiring at least 2 observations).
    *NSAMPLES* defaults to 1000. Returns "($lower_cl, $mean, $upper_cl)".
    Offers second-order accuracy for location statistics, though individual
    small-sample bootstrap replicates with zero sample variance can yield
    infinite endpoints.

  median_percentile_confidence_limits(SAMPLE, CONFIDENCE, [NSAMPLES])
    Calculates two-sided percentile bootstrap confidence limits for the
    exact median of *SAMPLE*. *NSAMPLES* defaults to 1000 (use 5000 or more
    for stable 95%+ limits). Returns "($lower_cl, $median, $upper_cl)".

  median_exact_confidence_limits(SAMPLE, CONFIDENCE)
    Calculates exact distribution-free confidence limits for the median of
    *SAMPLE* using the symmetric binomial distribution "Binomial(n, 0.5)" on
    the order statistics, returning "($lower_cl, $median, $upper_cl)" with
    at least *CONFIDENCE* coverage (assuming continuous i.i.d.
    observations). Achieving guaranteed coverage at level *CONFIDENCE*
    requires "1 - 2**(1 - N) >= CONFIDENCE" (for example, "N >= 6" at 95%
    confidence); smaller samples return "[min, max]". This requires no Monte
    Carlo resampling and runs in O(n) expected time.

  simple_confidence_limits_from_samples(STATISTIC, STATISTIC_SAMPLES, CONFIDENCE)
    Alias/equivalent to "basic_confidence_limits_from_samples(STATISTIC,
    STATISTIC_SAMPLES, CONFIDENCE)": calculates two-sided basic (pivotal)
    bootstrap confidence limits at level *CONFIDENCE* using "alpha/2" and "1
    - alpha/2" quantiles, returning "($lower_cl, $statistic, $upper_cl)".

    For example:

      my $sample = [<numbers>];
      my $means = resample_means($sample, 5000);
      my ($lower_cl, $mean, $upper_cl)
        = simple_confidence_limits_from_samples(mean($sample), $means, 0.95);

    If you want to apply this logic to other statistics such as the first or
    third quartile, you can use resample_quantiles():

      my $sample = [<numbers>];
      my $quartiles = resample_quantiles($sample, 0.25, 5000);
      my ($lower_cl, $firstq, $upper_cl)
        = percentile_confidence_limits_from_samples(
            first_quartile($sample), $quartiles, 0.95
          );

    For medians, prefer "median_exact_confidence_limits(SAMPLE, CONFIDENCE)"
    or "median_percentile_confidence_limits(SAMPLE, CONFIDENCE,
    [NSAMPLES])".

  median_simple_confidence_limits(SAMPLE, CONFIDENCE, [NSAMPLES])
    Calculates two-sided basic (pivotal) bootstrap confidence limits for the
    median of *SAMPLE* at level *CONFIDENCE* (using "alpha/2" and "1 -
    alpha/2" tails). Returns "($lower_cl, $median, $upper_cl)". *NSAMPLES*
    defaults to 1000.

    Note that basic (pivotal) bootstrap reflection can undercover on
    discrete/small-sample medians due to order-statistic spacing asymmetry;
    prefer "median_exact_confidence_limits(SAMPLE, CONFIDENCE)" or
    "median_percentile_confidence_limits(SAMPLE, CONFIDENCE, [NSAMPLES])"
    for medians.

OTHER FUNCTIONS
  approx_erf($x)
    Calculates the error function of *x* (using the C math library erf()
    where available, with a high-precision fallback otherwise).

  approx_erf_inv($x)
    Calculates the inverse of the error function of *x* for *x* in "(-1, 1)"
    to near machine precision.

  nsigma_to_alpha($nsigma)
    Calculates the two-sided probability "erfc($nsigma / sqrt(2))" that a
    measurement from a normal distribution is further away from the mean
    than $nsigma standard deviations, for "$nsigma >= 0".

    The two-sided confidence level is "1 - nsigma_to_alpha($nsigma)".

  alpha_to_nsigma($alpha)
    Inverse of nsigma_to_alpha() for $alpha in "(0, 1]".

  mean(ARRAYREF)
    Calculates the arithmetic mean of a sample using compensated summation
    (and overflow-safe scaling for extreme magnitudes).

  quantile(ARRAYREF, P)
    Calculates the sample quantile for probability *P* in "[0, 1]" in O(n)
    expected time ("O(n log n)" worst case) using Hyndman-Fan Type 7 linear
    interpolation (the default in R, NumPy, and Julia). At "P = 0.5", this
    computes the exact median, averaging the two middle elements when N is
    even.

  median(ARRAYREF)
    Calculates the median (second quartile) of a sample using
    "quantile($sample, 0.5)", averaging the two middle elements when N is
    even. Runs in O(n) expected time ("O(n log n)" worst case).

  median_absolute_deviation(ARRAYREF)
    Calculates the median absolute deviation (MAD) in O(n) expected time.
    Note that for consistency with a normal standard deviation, a normally
    distributed sample requires rescaling by "1 / (sqrt(2) * erfinv(0.5))"
    (approximately 1.4826).

  first_quartile(ARRAYREF)
    Calculates the first quartile of the sample using Hyndman-Fan Type 7
    interpolation (equivalent to "quantile($sample, 0.25)").

  third_quartile(ARRAYREF)
    Calculates the third quartile of the sample using Hyndman-Fan Type 7
    interpolation (equivalent to "quantile($sample, 0.75)").

  select_kth(ARRAYREF, K)
    Selects the kth smallest element (1-based) from the sample in O(n)
    expected time (hardened with introselect heapsort fallback for "O(n log
    n)" worst-case time).

  sample_standard_deviation([$mean,] ARRAYREF)
    Calculates the sample standard deviation (with Bessel's "N - 1"
    correction). May be called with either "(\@sample)" (computing the
    sample mean automatically) or "($mean, \@sample)". Note that Bessel's "N
    - 1" denominator is only statistically appropriate when $mean is the
    sample mean of @sample.

  population_standard_deviation([$mean,] ARRAYREF)
    Same as "sample_standard_deviation", but dividing by "N" instead of "N -
    1". May be called as "(\@sample)" or "($mean, \@sample)".

TODO
    One could calculate more statistics in C for performance.

SEE ALSO
    Math::Random::MT

    The selection implementation uses Hoare's quickselect with an
    introselect heapsort fallback to guarantee "O(n log n)" worst-case time
    while keeping O(n) expected runtime. See
    <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Introselect>.

